sophia: Heads-up: PR https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/548 adds a combined 'errors & fallbacks' doc but reviewers asked for runnable SDK examples (not just pseudocode). Can we add a Go snippet (context + backoff) and a short ruby snippet for web apps?
leo: Good call. A lot of customers are on backend Go stacks. Suggested pattern: context.WithTimeout, retry loop with backoff + jitter, and explicit client cancellation. Also show a lightweight fallback to a cached response or smaller model.
anya: I'll draft the Go + Ruby examples. Proposal: default 2 retries (so 3 attempts total), initial backoff 300ms, cap 1500ms, jitter +-100ms. Call out that compact models may have different prompt length behavior and might not support the same token budget.
jamal: Also add a one-line migration note: "SDKs >=0.9 provide built-in retry and timeout helpers; prefer those when available" and link to the SDK changelog in the PR description.
sophia: Add a short section on context limits: show two fallback choices — aggressive truncation vs. summarize-then-append. Explain tradeoffs in 1-2 bullets.
leo: For streaming endpoints: note that reconnects should request only missing continuation (use server sequence IDs) to avoid repeating long prompts. If that's not available, document best-effort dedupe heuristics.
anya: Draft Go snippet (will include):
```go
// example: context timeout + exponential backoff + fallback to compact model
package main

import (
    "context"
    "errors"
    "fmt"
    "math"
    "math/rand"
    "net/http"
    "time"
)

func callWithRetry(ctx context.Context, client *http.Client, url string, maxRetries int) ([]byte, error) {
    var lastErr error
    for i := 0; i <= maxRetries; i++ {
        attemptCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 5*time.Second)
        req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(attemptCtx, "POST", url, nil)
        resp, err := client.Do(req)
        cancel()
        if err == nil && resp.StatusCode < 500 && resp.StatusCode != 429 {
            // read body omitted for brevity
            return []byte("ok"), nil
        }
        if err != nil {
            lastErr = err
        } else if resp.StatusCode == 429 {
            lastErr = errors.New("rate_limited")
        } else {
            lastErr = fmt.Errorf("status:%d", resp.StatusCode)
        }
        if i == maxRetries { break }
        backoff := time.Duration(math.Min(1.5e9, 300e6*math.Pow(2, float64(i)))) // cap ~1.5s
        jitter := time.Duration((rand.Int63n(200)-100) * int64(time.Millisecond))
        select {
        case <-time.After(backoff + jitter):
        case <-ctx.Done():
            return nil, ctx.Err()
        }
    }
    return nil, lastErr
}

func generateWithFallback(ctx context.Context, client *http.Client, primary, compact string) ([]byte, error) {
    data, err := callWithRetry(ctx, client, primary, 2)
    if err == nil { return data, nil }
    // if rate limited or timeout, best-effort compact model
    if err.Error() == "rate_limited" || errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded) {
        return callWithRetry(ctx, client, compact, 1)
    }
    return nil, err
}
```
jamal: Nice. Add a short comment that switching models may invalidate KV cache and change token/latency characteristics.
anya: Ruby snippet (Rack/app example):
```ruby
# simple pattern: net/http with retries and cached fallback
require 'net/http'
require 'json'

def post_with_retry(uri_str, payload, max_retries=2)
  uri = URI(uri_str)
  attempts = 0
  begin
    req = Net::HTTP::Post.new(uri)
    req['Content-Type'] = 'application/json'
    req['Authorization'] = "Bearer #{ENV['REDWOOD_KEY']}"
    req.body = payload.to_json
    res = Net::HTTP.start(uri.hostname, uri.port, use_ssl: uri.scheme == 'https') do |http|
      http.request(req)
    end
    raise 'RATE_LIMIT' if res.code.to_i == 429
    return JSON.parse(res.body)
  rescue => e
    attempts += 1
    raise if attempts > max_retries
    sleep 0.3 * (2 ** attempts)
    retry
  end
end

# fallback to cache
def generate_or_cached(primary, compact, payload, cache)
  begin
    return post_with_retry(primary, payload)
  rescue => e
    return cache[:last_good] if cache[:last_good]
    return post_with_retry(compact, payload)
  end
end
```
leo: Looks good. One tiny nit: in Go sample, don't ignore resp.Body in real code; add a comment to that effect. Also include brief nginx/ingress guidance: client-side timeout ~= backend timeout - 500ms so there is slack for retries/cleanup.
sophia: We should also add a 1-line ops checklist at the end: update alert thresholds, add a runbook link, confirm redirects for old doc slugs, and include sample telemetry events (rate_limit, fallback_used, compact_model_fallback).
